{:edb/db-before-t 972 :edb/db-after-t 973 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534285 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:35:34.216Z" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115078 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/0a/0a034d98da82572a523d406379ab286d1af6b37820f5c2380822abf611643f83.png)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115078 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/b3/b34f8d0d2c5f05f6c104395e75e9f5c93df7812155e09bc47828747e752d003a.svg)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115079 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/89/89189584fab0cbb79fb0b4ac30a81b41bfd6508de7499c111fd53fb6c0c2c928.png)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115079 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/91/919674aa42fd55c536318518d9e14de07a515d3c33cb7868378ce168221f13ff.svg)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115080 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](images/e0/e034f65af225ce46015a5accae774a24eed8617590a583a2a885b4b1c5332103.svg)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115080 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](images/e6/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417379 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417378 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115474 1050 420.0 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113352 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114037 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417376 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115850 1071 "The inverse of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186115850 1071 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186115850 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186116498 1064 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186116498 1064 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n> *The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534285 17592186045417 false] [17592186417360 1080 "$B$ only" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417360 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417360 1083 #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417361 1080 "$B$ and $C$ only" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417361 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417361 1083 #uuid "0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417362 1080 "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417362 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417362 1083 #uuid "27195729-1b10-59a4-9a7d-c8f3fb5b730d" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417363 1080 "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417363 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534285 17592186045417 true] [17592186417363 1083 #uuid "30ffe217-9ca4-5886-8537-db